% Disk I/O: File Creation and Storage
% Chapter 8 of the literate BitTorrent client

\section{Disk I/O}

Before downloading any piece data, we must prepare the output files. BitTorrent
torrents can contain single files or entire directory trees, and our disk module
handles creating this structure on the local filesystem. The key operation is
creating \emph{sparse files}---files that reserve space in the filesystem
metadata but don't actually allocate disk blocks until data is written.

\subsection{Why Sparse Files?}

When downloading a 4 GB file, we don't want to immediately allocate 4 GB of
disk space filled with zeros. Instead, we create a file with the correct
reported size but no actual data blocks. As pieces are downloaded and verified,
we write them to specific offsets within this sparse file. The operating system
allocates disk blocks only for the regions we actually write to.

This approach has several benefits:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Fast initialization}: Creating a sparse file is nearly instant
  regardless of size, since we're only updating filesystem metadata.
\item \textbf{Efficient disk usage}: Partially downloaded torrents consume only
  as much space as the data actually received.
\item \textbf{Resume support}: If we resume a download, the already-written
  regions remain on disk while gaps remain sparse.
\end{itemize}

Most modern filesystems (ext4, NTFS, APFS, Btrfs) support sparse files
transparently. The \texttt{File::set\_len} method in Rust creates sparse files
on these systems---it sets the file size in metadata without writing any data.

\subsection{Error Handling}

Disk operations can fail for many reasons: permission denied, disk full,
filesystem errors, or invalid paths. We define a dedicated error type that
captures the file path involved, making debugging straightforward.

<<disk errors>>=
use std::path::PathBuf;

/// Errors that can occur during disk I/O operations.
///
/// Unlike generic I/O errors, these include the path context so that
/// error messages clearly identify which file caused the problem.
#[derive(Debug, thiserror::Error)]
pub enum DiskError {
    /// Failed to create a directory.
    ///
    /// This can happen if:
    /// - The parent directory doesn't exist and we lack permission to create it
    /// - A file already exists at this path
    /// - The path contains invalid characters
    #[error("failed to create directory {path}: {source}")]
    CreateDirectory {
        /// The directory path we tried to create.
        path: PathBuf,
        /// The underlying I/O error.
        #[source]
        source: std::io::Error,
    },

    /// Failed to create or open a file.
    ///
    /// This can happen if:
    /// - The parent directory doesn't exist
    /// - Permission is denied
    /// - A directory exists at this path
    #[error("failed to create file {path}: {source}")]
    CreateFile {
        /// The file path we tried to create.
        path: PathBuf,
        /// The underlying I/O error.
        #[source]
        source: std::io::Error,
    },

    /// Failed to set file length (sparse allocation).
    ///
    /// This can happen if:
    /// - The filesystem doesn't support sparse files (rare)
    /// - The filesystem is full (no space for metadata)
    /// - Permission is denied
    #[error("failed to set file size for {path}: {source}")]
    SetFileSize {
        /// The file path we tried to resize.
        path: PathBuf,
        /// The underlying I/O error.
        #[source]
        source: std::io::Error,
    },

    /// Failed to get file metadata.
    ///
    /// This can happen if:
    /// - The file was deleted between operations
    /// - Permission is denied
    #[error("failed to get metadata for {path}: {source}")]
    Metadata {
        /// The file path we tried to query.
        path: PathBuf,
        /// The underlying I/O error.
        #[source]
        source: std::io::Error,
    },

<<disk write error>>
}
@

The error variants are specific to each operation rather than having a single
generic ``disk error.'' This specificity helps debugging: knowing whether we
failed to create a directory vs. set a file size points to different root
causes (permissions vs. filesystem support vs. disk space).

\subsection{Creating Output Files}

The main entry point for file creation is [[create\_output\_files]]. Given an
output directory and a list of [[FileInfo]] structures from the metainfo, it:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Creates all necessary parent directories
\item Creates each file as a sparse file with the correct size
\item Handles idempotency: existing files are left unchanged
\end{enumerate}

The idempotency requirement is important for resumed downloads. If the user
restarts the client after a partial download, we don't want to truncate files
that already contain verified piece data.

<<create output files>>=
use std::fs::{self, OpenOptions};
use std::path::Path;

use crate::metainfo::FileInfo;

/// Creates all output files for a torrent.
///
/// This function prepares the filesystem for receiving piece data by creating
/// the directory structure and sparse files specified in the torrent metadata.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `output_dir` - Base directory where files will be created
/// * `files` - List of files from the torrent's metainfo
///
/// # Sparse Files
///
/// Files are created as sparse files using `set_len`. This means the reported
/// file size matches the expected size, but no disk space is allocated until
/// data is actually written. This enables:
///
/// - Fast initialization regardless of file size
/// - Efficient partial downloads
/// - Resume support without re-creating files
///
/// # Idempotency
///
/// This function is safe to call multiple times:
/// - Existing directories are not modified
/// - Existing files with the correct size are not touched
/// - Existing files with incorrect size are NOT truncated (to preserve data)
///
/// # Errors
///
/// Returns `DiskError` if:
/// - A directory cannot be created (permission denied, path conflict)
/// - A file cannot be created (permission denied, path is a directory)
/// - File size cannot be set (filesystem limitations)
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```ignore
/// use literate_bittorrent::disk::create_output_files;
/// use literate_bittorrent::metainfo::FileInfo;
/// use std::path::PathBuf;
///
/// let files = vec![
///     FileInfo { length: 1024, path: PathBuf::from("torrent_name/file1.txt") },
///     FileInfo { length: 2048, path: PathBuf::from("torrent_name/subdir/file2.txt") },
/// ];
///
/// create_output_files(Path::new("/downloads"), &files)?;
/// // Creates:
/// //   /downloads/torrent_name/file1.txt (1024 bytes, sparse)
/// //   /downloads/torrent_name/subdir/file2.txt (2048 bytes, sparse)
/// ```
pub fn create_output_files(output_dir: &Path, files: &[FileInfo]) -> Result<(), DiskError> {
    for file_info in files {
        let full_path = output_dir.join(&file_info.path);

        // Create parent directories if needed
        if let Some(parent) = full_path.parent() {
            if !parent.exists() {
                fs::create_dir_all(parent).map_err(|e| DiskError::CreateDirectory {
                    path: parent.to_path_buf(),
                    source: e,
                })?;
            }
        }

        // Check if file already exists with correct size
        if full_path.exists() {
            let metadata = fs::metadata(&full_path).map_err(|e| DiskError::Metadata {
                path: full_path.clone(),
                source: e,
            })?;

            // If file exists with correct size, skip it (idempotent)
            if metadata.len() == file_info.length {
                continue;
            }

            // File exists but wrong size - we don't truncate to preserve data
            // The file will be overwritten piece by piece during download
            // This handles the case where a previous download was interrupted
            // and we're resuming with potentially verified pieces
            continue;
        }

        // Create the file
        let file = OpenOptions::new()
            .write(true)
            .create(true)
            .truncate(false) // Don't truncate if somehow file was created between check and open
            .open(&full_path)
            .map_err(|e| DiskError::CreateFile {
                path: full_path.clone(),
                source: e,
            })?;

        // Set file size (sparse allocation)
        file.set_len(file_info.length).map_err(|e| DiskError::SetFileSize {
            path: full_path.clone(),
            source: e,
        })?;
    }

    Ok(())
}
@

The implementation handles several edge cases:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Parent directory creation}: We use [[create\_dir\_all]] which
  works like [[mkdir -p]]---it creates all missing parent directories in one
  call. If the directory already exists, this is a no-op.

\item \textbf{Existing files}: If a file already exists with the correct size,
  we skip it entirely. This preserves any data from previous downloads. If the
  size is wrong, we still don't truncate---the piece-writing code will overwrite
  the relevant portions.

\item \textbf{Race conditions}: We check existence before creating, but another
  process could create the file between our check and our [[open]] call. Using
  [[truncate(false)]] ensures we don't accidentally destroy data in this case.

\item \textbf{Zero-length files}: BitTorrent allows zero-length files (common
  for placeholder files in directory structures). [[set\_len(0)]] on a newly
  created file is a no-op but harmless.
\end{itemize}

